## Verification

### What is checked

| Test | Method | Scope |
|------|--------|-------|
| Tiny random graphs | Exhaustive subset enumeration **and** Edmonds–Karp | every pair, 300 graphs, `n = 2..9` |
| Random sparse subgraphs | Independent Edmonds–Karp max-flow | every pair, 40 graphs, `n = 20..79`, caps up to `1e12` |
| Edge cases | Edmonds–Karp | parallel edges, self-loops, disconnected, `1e12` caps, zero caps |
| Scale test | Timing + spot checks vs Edmonds–Karp | `n = 2000`, `m = 20000`, caps up to `1e12` |

Edmonds–Karp is a deliberately different algorithm (BFS augmenting paths, no level graph, no scaling), so agreement is strong evidence of correctness. Brute force enumerates all `2^n` cuts, giving an implementation-independent ground truth on tiny inputs.

